            <ArticleTitle>Annular keratotomy for the treatment of painful bullous keratopathy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>93-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine whether annular keratotomy can relieve pain associated with bullous keratopathy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Three patients with painful bullous keratopathy and poor visual potential underwent partial-thickness (0.30 to 0.42 mm) trephination of the cornea under local anesthesia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All patients reported an absence of pain and foreign body sensation during an average follow-up interval of six months. The surgical procedure was uncomplicated; however, two eyes developed increased central corneal stromal edema.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Annular keratotomy appears to be a relatively safe and simple surgical procedure that may be useful in managing pain in eyes with painful cornea edema and poor visual potential.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
